WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.780
that to target specialty groups which have a higher propensity to want to test and treat.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:11.740
So those are, of course, cardiovascular specialty groups, but also when you think about

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.920
interventional cardiology, I mean, there are certain specialty groups we're learning that

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:22.480
have a higher propensity to look for these biomarkers and then treat in order to avoid

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.860
the recurrence of events, also given the push towards healthcare quality around the world.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.760
So we're trying to take a very targeted approach in how we think about our LP little a launch.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.660
And in the future as well, in cardiovascular launches in general, take a more specialty

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:41.200
mindset which allows us to target to the right patients and the right physicians

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.360
with better resource allocation and hopefully drive more rapid uptake in the future.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.400
Thank you, Richard.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.000
Thank you.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:51.240
Thank you.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:56.240
Your next question comes from the line of Graham Parry, Bank of America.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.240
Please go ahead.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:00:59.020
Great.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:00:59.840
Thanks for taking my questions.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.460
Just wanted to come back to Natalie, actually.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.480
Can you just clarify, you still think there's no manufacturing site inspection needed for

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.240
the new process?

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.180
I think you said on Q1 you didn't expect to see one.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.140
I assume you'd actually know by now.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:16.960
And just, again, that you remain confident that there is no adcom coming and confidence

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.220
in the broad label.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.020
I know there's some discussion in the market about the node negative patient population

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:24.660
and whether that's approvable or not.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.140
Thank you.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.380
Yeah, thanks, Graham.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.060
Again, based on the label discussions that we have, we're confident in the broad label

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.840
and there is no manufacturing inspection.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.140
I mean, all of the changes we've made are all related to product handling.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.140
And so I think, and some of the suppliers in the system.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.360
So there's no inspections.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.040
This is just providing stability data, which we're always obligated to do when we make

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:51.480
changes.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.980
Once we finish, provide that stability data and have adequate stability data.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.840
That's where the FDA takes their decision.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.440
So it's really a topic of finalizing that review of our stability data from an FDA standpoint.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.080
But we've put a little comment in the broad label, no other inspections.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.160
And we're gearing up for a launch in late Q3.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.580
Next question, operator.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.120
Thank you.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.440
Your next question comes from the line of James Quigley from Goldman Sachs.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:24.300
Please go ahead.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:26.920
Great.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:27.760
Thank you for taking my questions.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.440
I've got one on.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.500
Could you walk us through some of the competitive dynamics you're seeing in the U.S.?

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.360
I mean, you mentioned some of the centers had 30% share.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:41.880
So what is the considerations there in terms of driving the increase in share in those

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.180
centers?

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.860
And then thinking also about the community centers, where are you in terms of the development

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.280
of the community centers and Plovicto and the offering there?

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:59.140
And when you speak to docs, physicians in those centers, how are they thinking about

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.940
Plovicto, which is relatively more complicated than the androgen receptor inhibitors, which

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.780
are overall simple and seem to be launching quite strongly as well?

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:07.380
Thank you.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.260
Yeah, thanks, James.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:15.560
When you look at the dynamics on Plovicto, so we have, let's say, roughly 425 centers.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:20.000
And about a third of those centers, we were really well established.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:25.260
In the vision population, we see 90% market share, so really high levels of share of the

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:25.860
vision patients.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.100
Then we have another group of centers, which are still earlier on in their evolution.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:31.460
We see about 50% share.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.480
Our goal is to get them to 90%.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.260
The last third of centers are much more in the community.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.580
And here, the dynamics are where we have to just do more work.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.360
A lot of it is education.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:47.240
So physicians understand, rather than cycling through, in the case of the vision population,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:54.400
cycling through chemo and perhaps doing extra rounds of chemo, better to refer and have

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:59.040
the patients receive Plovicto, given the compelling results that we saw in the vision population.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:04.380
So in order to motivate that, we're doing a few things, as I mentioned, another field

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:10.540
force to get out there to educate community oncology, community urology, and as well to

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.140
strengthen that referral base.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:17.960
Second, DTC to make sure patients understand in the community that there is this option,

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.760
so rather than cycling through chemo.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.860
Earlier on, where we know the earlier patients get Plovicto, the better the outcomes to

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:25.840
try to motivate that.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.800
And I think if we can unlock that segment.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.000
Over the course of the coming months, we can continue to drive Plovicto in the vision

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.620
population to the multi-billion or roughly $2 billion potential we think there is.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:40.960
And that includes globally.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.520
I would say we're seeing very robust uptake in Germany.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.860
And over time, we expect robust uptake in other European markets.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:52.940
Now, all of that is important for the current indication, but even more important as we

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.240
move to PSMA4, where we have three times the patient population.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.780
And there, again, it will be.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.300
It will be really important to, over time, build up confidence in the community to be

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.840
able, outside of the main medical centers, to provide radioligant therapy, not only for

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:14.930
Plovicto, but a portfolio of radioligant.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:19.250
So we're confident we're going to get there step by step, but it will take time.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.790
I think things like the patient-ready dose will help.

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:22.910
I think also.

